                <h2>The Scene: Two Weeks Before Black Friday, 60% of Budget Gone with Half the Conversions</h2>
<p>Lisa, a tool niche independent site seller, reached us in a panic. Her situation:</p>
<ul>
<li>Daily budget $3,000 across Facebook and Google</li>
<li>$42,000 already spent, but CPA was 47% higher than usual</li>
<li>ROAS dropped from 1.9 to 0.8</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>"We thought it was a creative problem, so we rushed out three new main creatives. No improvement at all."</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Lisa's team spent days iterating ad creatives while missing the real issue—<strong>structural budget misallocation</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Diagnosis: Three Hidden Budget Traps</h2>
<p><strong>1. Channel Allocation Imbalance</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Facebook: 65% (CPM up 120% during peak season)</li>
<li>Google Search: 25% (overbidding on core keywords)</li>
<li>Smart Shopping: 10% (pulled before cold-start completion)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. Severe Audience Overlap</strong></p>
<p>Multiple ad sets targeting the same users, causing ad fatigue. Three flagship products had 68% audience overlap.</p>
<p><strong>3. No Dayparting Strategy</strong></p>
<p>Uniform bidding 24/7 wasted ~22% of budget during off-peak hours.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Actions: Emergency Adjustments in 3 Days</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Reprioritize Channels</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Facebook</strong>: Reduced to 40%, paused low performers, focused on exclusive holiday creatives and retargeting</li>
<li><strong>Google DSA</strong>: Increased to 35%, enabled brand protection + competitor blocking</li>
<li><strong>Smart Shopping</strong>: Shrunk to 10%, given 14-day learning window</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Audience Isolation</h3>
<p>Each product line got exclusive audiences—no overlap allowed. One user = one product touchpoint at a time.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Dayparting + Budget Cap</h3>
<ul>
<li>High-converting hours: 9am-12pm target market → 1.3x bid</li>
<li>Low-efficiency hours: 12am-6am → 0.5x bid</li>
<li>Budget allocation rules triggered at 80% daily threshold</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Results: 14 Days to ROAS 0.8→2.3</h2>
<table>
